From c6cf36e8b019dd97eeb842017062039f520dc3ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: D-Ogi <6542335+D-Ogi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 20:48:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] radv/wddm2: bind queues to runtime queue contexts through
 a private entry

vkCreateInstance now fills struct bc250_host_queue_funcs with
bind(VkQueue, cookie) and unbind(VkQueue). This is the way the shell
already reaches hosted-only RADV: a private structure on a standard
call, no Vulkan extension. The engine's BindQueue and UnbindQueue
services (ABI 1.1 draft, V7) call them on the thread of the engine
call, with the queue cookie, or NULL for the internal queue.

Two host operations carry the queue scope. CREATE_QUEUE_CONTEXT (6)
and DESTROY_QUEUE_CONTEXT (7) take struct bc250_host_queue_context:
the cookie and the D3DKMT arguments, so the shell can call
pfnCreateContextVirtualCb and pfnDestroyContextCb with the
hRTCommandQueue that the cookie names. They are made only inside bind
and unbind. The monitored fences stay device-scoped
(CreateSynchronizationObject2).

ctx_bind refuses a lost device and a bound context, creates the
context through the cookie, then the progress and companion fences,
and on any failure releases what it created within the call, the
context through the same cookie. The queue can be bound again. Every
bind starts from zeroed queue state, so fence values and gather slot
retirement never carry over from an earlier context.

ctx_unbind waits, bounded and not on a lost device, for the queue's
own progress fence and companion fence. Once both retired it releases
the progress fence, the gather BOs, the companion fence and the
context, in that order, and returns VK_SUCCESS. Afterwards no host call
names the context or the cookie.

Nothing is released on a guess. When the wait fails, times out or
finds the device lost, the work may still run: unbind destroys and
forgets nothing, the context included, returns VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST
and RADV marks the queue lost. When the host fails a destroy (a fence,
operation 7, a hardware queue), that object stays with the queue,
handle and all, and unbind returns VK_ERROR_UNKNOWN. A failed bind
whose cleanup fails is treated the same way. Such a queue is never
bound again and makes no further host call; what it kept is left to
the host's device, and vkDestroyDevice makes no call for it. The same
checks now apply to the device-level contexts of other instances,
where only their failure paths change.

A context still bound at vkDestroyDevice is abandoned: it is never
destroyed through a cookie that may be stale. The device-scoped fences
and BOs are released if its work retired, and kept otherwise.

bind refuses a queue that is not bindable, a lost device, a queue
whose submit mode is not immediate and a queue already bound. Both
entries take the queue lock that submission takes.

The ABI and the bind contract remain a draft pending T0.
